WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.170
The crowded theatrical release calendar of
2020 was bound to produce box office disappointments.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.800
Some are massive, star-packed tentpole films
that audiences weren't interested in.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.530
Others are smaller films with bad word-of-mouth.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:16.970
However they got here, these are the biggest
flops of 2020 so far.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:20.600
Sometimes a film just doesn't land, even when
it has its defenders.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.880
That was the case with Underwater, the Kristen
Stewart-led sci-fi horror film that was presented

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.929
as a kind of creature feature throwback movie
in the vein of films like Alien and The Abyss.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:34.280
The film arrived at a slow time for films
anyway, in early January, and while it certainly

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.860
had its fair share of critical fans early
on, it garnered mixed reviews.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.480
Then, audiences just didn't seem to want to
show up, and a C CinemaScore on opening weekend

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.520
didn't help with that.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.989
"We drilled to the bottom of the ocean…
and we don't know what came out."

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:52.399
Underwater picked up just $7 million in the
U.S. on its opening weekend, on its way to

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:55.650
a domestic total of $17.2 million.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:00.270
The film did better internationally, grossing
$23.5 million in various territories around

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:01.270
the world.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.489
The eventual total, as of mid-March, is just
over $40 million.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.960
A lot of modestly priced horror films would
love to have a worldwide total like that,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.320
but Underwater wasn't modestly priced.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:16.000
Its reported budget was closer to $60 million
thanks to its effects-heavy production, and

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.130
that meant the box office was significantly
underwhelming.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:23.630
Dolittle, an adaptation of Hugh Lofting's
Doctor Dolittle stories, was Robert Downey

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:28.180
Jr.'s first major project since his exit from
the ultra-profitable Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.450
he reigned over for more than a decade.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.810
It was a longtime passion project of Downey's,
a film packed with stars in its voice cast,

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:39.400
and an attempt for the MCU godfather to use
his clout to deliver some magical family fun

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.759
at the box office.

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:45.380
Unfortunately, Dolittle was a troubled production,
as various rewrites and reshoots led to the

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.130
budget swelling to a massive $175 million.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:52.790
That made it tough to recoup costs in the
first place, but then audiences just didn't

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:56.270
seem to want to show up for the film, and
critics savaged it.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:00.189
Downey's first major post-Marvel tentpole
deflated rapidly, and while there was some

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:04.119
hope that it could gain some ground in China,
the coronavirus outbreak there led to its

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:05.760
release getting cancelled.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.260
Dolittle bottomed out at about $223.3 million
worldwide, which was likely not enough to

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.780
earn back the cost of promoting and producing
the film.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.270
Horror films are often protected from box
office disaster in a way that others films

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.270
aren't.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.239
For one thing, there are a lot of people who
just like to see whatever the latest horror

00:02:22.239 --> 00:02:25.290
film is, regardless of what critics might
say about it.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.110
For another, horror films are usually at least
relatively cheap to make.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.690
Put out a film that cost $10 million, earn
$40 million worldwide, turn a profit, and

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:34.690
move on.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:38.360
Then there are those films that just can't
quite overcome lackluster marketing and poor

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.840
word-of-mouth response, like The Turning.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.519
"Geez, your friends must love it here."

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:44.690
"I have no friends."

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.870
"You have no friends?"

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:46.870
"Yup."

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:47.870
"Well, you have one now."

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.050
"Hah hah…

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.050
Maybe."

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.599
This modern-day retelling of Henry James'
Turn of the Screw was perhaps fighting an

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:57.970
uphill battle to begin with thanks to its
January release date and pushbacks on the

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.360
release calendar.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.290
Then came the early response.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.590
Critics didn't like the film, and audiences
didn't respond to it either.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:09.200
The Turning, in part because of its disappointing
ending, earned a rare "F" CinemaScore grade

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.190
on its opening weekend, and everyone pretty
much stayed away after that.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:18.989
On a budget of $14 million, the film has earned
just $18.4 million worldwide.

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:22.521
The Rhythm Section, a Blake Lively-led revenge
thriller in the vein of films like Taken,

00:03:22.521 --> 00:03:26.599
seemed to have potential to be a new badass
female-driven action hit.

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:30.340
The keyword there is "seemed," because the
reality of the film's release ultimately spelled

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:35.110
one of the worst box office duds ever recorded,
with a number of contributing factors.

00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:39.520
The film opened at the end of January and
grossed just $2.7 million domestically in

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.909
its first weekend.

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:45.630
That's the lowest box office haul for a film
opening in more than 3,000 theaters ever,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.099
and one of the lowest openings of all time
for a major studio release.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:51.159
So, what went wrong?

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:55.770
According to Deadline, it began with lackluster
test screenings that basically convinced executives

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:59.580
at Paramount that the film wasn't worth pouring
a ton of marketing money into.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.819
Add in a lackluster critical response and
an opening weekend CinemaScore of C+, and

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:07.819
it was a film that had no real buzz and no
word of mouth to speak of.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:11.939
The film's lukewarm domestic performance meant
that it barely got an international release

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:18.380
at all, and the box office total came to just
$5.9 million, losing upwards of $30 million.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:23.050
While it may seem like they rule the box office
every year thanks to everything from superhero

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:27.790
tentpoles to live-action remakes of animated
classics, even Disney produces a bomb now

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:28.790
and then.

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.530
Their latest major box office disappointment,
which the studio inherited in their purchase

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:37.000
of Fox, came in the form of The Call of the
Wild, the Harrison Ford-led adaptation of

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.570
Jack London's novel that became known rather
quickly as "that movie where Harrison Ford

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.590
hangs out with a CGI dog."

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.280
"That's mine!

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.980
Hey, thanks."

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:56.660
The film's decision to use a motion-captured,
CGI dog to play Ford's canine companion rather

00:04:56.660 --> 00:05:02.470
than a real, trained animal helped to swell
the film's budget to a hefty $135 million.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:06.380
That's a steep box office hill to climb, and
audiences didn't seem ready to help the film

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:07.789
reach the summit.

00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:12.430
The Call of the Wild pulled in less than $25
million domestically in its opening weekend,

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.710
for a worldwide total of $107.5 million as
of mid-March.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:22.460
In March 2020, box office experts estimated
the film could end up losing as much as $50

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.740
million.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.420
Even under normal circumstances, some films
just don't do well at the box office.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.539
By the middle of March 2020, though, it became
clear that movies this year would be dealing

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:34.080
with anything but normal circumstances.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.160
As the coronavirus pandemic swept across the
world, movie studios and theater chains had

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.470
to make some difficult decisions.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.880
Releases were delayed indefinitely, while
theaters began closing to limit the spread

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.090
of the virus.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:48.759
While several upcoming blockbusters had their
release dates postponed to wait for a more

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:53.780
opportune time, a few films already in theaters
had their box office runs severely shortened.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.410
Onward, Pixar's latest major release, had
a solid week or so on screens, pulling in

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:04.419
$102.7 million worldwide, not bad for a start,
but still well short of the total the studio

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:05.760
may have hoped for.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.810
Meanwhile, Sony's superhero adventure Bloodshot
managed $24.2 million after a March 13 opening,

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:15.660
at a time when many people were already avoiding
crowded theaters.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.860
Then there's The Hunt, a film that had already
been delayed once, which pulled in just $6.3

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.370
million after its own March 13 opening, well
below its budget.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.130
It's possible that future theatrical time
or home releases can help studios gain some

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:32.210
ground on these films, but again, it's just
a case of bad timing.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.070
We may never know how high any of these films
might have actually climbed.

